Here byte and character streams write content two different ways — raw bytes versus encoded text.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;

public class ByteVsCharStreams {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        // Byte stream: binary-safe, no character decoding
        try (var out = new FileOutputStream("data.bin")) {
            out.write(new byte[]{1, 2, 3});
        }
        // Character stream: text plus an explicit charset
        try (var w = new OutputStreamWriter(
                new FileOutputStream("note.txt"), StandardCharsets.UTF_8)) {
            w.write("hello world"); // encoded as UTF-8 bytes
        }
    }
}
```

The byte stream stores three exact bytes, while the character writer encodes the text using the explicit charset, so accented letters survive the round trip.

This stack reads a UTF-8 properties file line by line, then shows the one-line modern equivalent.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.nio.file.*;

public class WrappingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        // Classic wrapping: file -> charset -> buffer
        try (var fileInput = new FileInputStream("application.properties");
             var reader = new InputStreamReader(fileInput, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
             var buffered = new BufferedReader(reader)) {
            String line;
            while ((line = buffered.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        }
        // Modern java.nio.file approach - one line
        var lines = Files.readAllLines(
                Path.of("application.properties"), StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
        System.out.println("lines: " + lines.size());
    }
}
```

The three-layer chain shows the pattern explicitly, but Files.readAllLines does the same wrapping for you behind the scenes.

These three patterns cover single-byte reads, fast bulk copying, and line-by-line text reading.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.*;

public class ReadWritePatterns {
    // Bulk read with byte array - EFFICIENT
    public void copyFile(Path source, Path target) throws IOException {
        try (var input = Files.newInputStream(source);
             var output = Files.newOutputStream(target)) {
            var buffer = new byte[8192]; // 8KB buffer
            int bytesRead;
            while ((bytesRead = input.read(buffer)) != -1) {
                output.write(buffer, 0, bytesRead); // only bytes actually read
            }
        }
    }

    // BufferedReader for text line-by-line
    public List<String> readLogFile(Path logFile) throws IOException {
        var lines = new ArrayList<String>();
        try (var reader = Files.newBufferedReader(logFile)) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                lines.add(line);
            }
        }
        return lines;
    }
}
```

The copy loop writes only bytesRead, never the full buffer, because the final chunk usually fills the array only partway.

```java
// If process() throws, in is NEVER closed -> descriptor leak
FileInputStream in = new FileInputStream("data.bin");
int b = in.read();
process(b);          // throws here -> close() below is skipped
in.close();
```

```java
// close() is guaranteed, even when the body throws
try (FileInputStream in = new FileInputStream("data.bin")) {
    int b = in.read();
    process(b);      // in is closed automatically as the block exits
}
```
*The resource is declared in the try header, so Java closes it on every exit path — normal or exceptional.*

Try-with-resources also handles several streams at once, closing them in reverse order.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

public class CopyWithLogging {
    // Multiple resources - closed in reverse order, even on exception
    public void copyFileWithLogging(Path source, Path target, Path logFile)
            throws IOException {
        try (var input = Files.newInputStream(source);
             var output = Files.newOutputStream(target);
             var logger = Files.newBufferedWriter(logFile)) {
            input.transferTo(output);
            logger.write("Copied " + source + " to " + target);
        } // closes logger, then output, then input
    }
}
```

The three resources close in the reverse of the order they opened, so the writer flushes its log before the streams it depends on go away.

The same read-and-write task shrinks dramatically when you move from java.io streams to the Files helpers.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

public class IoVsNio {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        // java.io: stream-oriented, manual loop
        try (var r = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("a.txt"))) {
            System.out.println(r.readLine());
        }
        // java.nio (NIO.2 Files): concise, exception-rich utilities
        String text = Files.readString(Path.of("a.txt"));
        Files.writeString(Path.of("b.txt"), text);
        System.out.println(text);
    }
}
```

Both read the same file, but Files.readString collapses the open-read-close dance into a single, well-tested line.
